          "status": "HYBRID",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s00163-024-00441-x?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s00163-024-00441-x, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence was built on the promise of human understanding and a construct of intelligence, whereas generative artificial intelligence (denoted as GenAI in the rest of the paper) is \"the science which studies the (fully) automated construction of intelligence\" (Zant et al. 2013). More pragmatically and mathematically, GenAI is a set of models designed to learn the hidden underlying structure of a dataset and to \"generate new data points that plausibly could be part of the original dataset\" (Pinaya et al. 2023). The definition of a model itself, both in GenAI and more generally in artificial intelligence, is not straightforward, even though authors have considered that a model is a combination of an architecture and a training phase for decades. This means that a model is obtained once its architecture and parameters are fixed, which is achieved through a training phase (Friedman et al. 2001), as is the case with famous neural networks. Following this, the training phase is part of the modeling process; as such, in the rest of the paper, we refer to a model once its training process is completed, its parameters are fixed, and it is thus able to make predictions or generate content (Ghahramani 2015). \n\nThe recent rise of ChatGPT, developed by OpenAI, opened an avenue for discussions concerning GenAI, both in the academic and public worlds. Mathematics and computer science scholars have focused on models and their technical performance, improving the existing models and developing new generative modeling frameworks (Gozalo-Brizuela and Garrido-Merchan 2023). The high promised and experienced performance of these models is leading to great hope for many use cases across various disciplines (Ooi 2023), such as medicine (Paladugu et al. 2023), supply chains (Fosso Wamba et al. 2023) and education (Baiḋoo-Anu and Ansah 2023), with more general promises regarding the benefits of GenAI for helping humanity solve great challenges such as climate change (Rane 2023). \n\nThese hopes are supported by several GenAI models with outstanding performance, as they benefit from continuous improvements provided by computer science scholars.",
