      "snippet": {
        "text": "For example, Crespo and Sinclair (2008) found that prospective teachers experienced difficulty engaging with problem posing due to their lack of familiarity with it in their roles as both students and educators [33]. \n\nAdditionally, the quality of posed problems is not always high, as demonstrated by Cai and Hwang (2002) [34] and Silver and Cai (1996) [35]. These findings underscore the need for further research into how educators learn to incorporate problem posing into their instruction and how they can improve the quality of their own problem sets in order to effectively integrate them into their teaching [36]. \n\nC. Large language models in education \n\nThe ChatGPT system [37] use in this work is based on a Large Language Model (LLM). LLMs are neural networks for natural language processing (NLP) that are trained on extensive text datasets and capable of generating humanlike text for a variety of language-related tasks [8]. Models such as GPT [38], BERT [39], and RoBERTa [40] have revolutionized the field of NLP and expanded the possibilities for research and applications. In education, these models can be integrated with chatbots to create adaptive and personalized learning experiences for students while supporting educators in their roles [41,42]. LLMs have been employed to assist students in numerous ways, including content generation [43,44], improving question-asking skills [45], generating code explanations [46], automating assessments [47], and providing feedback in language learning [48]. Chatbots can serve as conversational partners, supporting students experiencing foreign language anxiety [49] or low communication readiness [50]. The integration of LLMs and chatbots can facilitate more engaging learning experiences and aid students in expressing curiosity and comprehending complex concepts [45]. Research indicates that educators hold positive attitudes towards AI in education [51], with factors such as perceived usefulness, ease of use, and trust in AI-based tools influencing their acceptance [52]. \n\nDespite the potential of AI and chatbots in education, several challenges and open questions remain to be addressed. The responsible integration of AI into education will require the collaborative efforts of diverse communities, including educators, researchers, and policymakers [53].",
