          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.02993, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "The emergence of generative AI, as represented by ChatGPT, has fundamentally transformed the way students interact with AI systems. Unlike earlier rule-based or menu-driven interfaces, generative AI supports natural, flexible, and text-based conversations, lowering the technical threshold for meaningful engagement. Recent research has identified a range of educational uses of generative AI, such as brainstorming, exploring new ideas, and problem-solving (Baı̇doo-Anu & Ansah, 2023;Pesovski et al., 2024;Šedlbauer, et al., 2024), thereby broadening the scope of AI applications and introducing new paradigms of student-AI interaction. \n\nInvestigations into the interactions between students and generative AI have begun to shed light on the distinct characteristics and outcomes of integrating AI in educational settings. A growing body of literature has focused on the functionalities of generative AI, such as ChatGPT, in assisting student learning. Recent reviews have categorized student-AI interactions into four predominant themes based on the roles and functions of AI: (1) authoritative resource, providing accurate and comprehensive responses; (2) facilitator, supporting self-directed learning by guiding learners through reflective processes; (3) collaborator, fostering collaborative dialogues that promote knowledge co-creation; and \n\n(4) extended cognitive capacity, enhancing students' intellectual engagement. These roles have been linked to positive outcomes across various domains, including language learning (Tai & Chen, 2024;Chen & Pan, 2022), programming (Denny et al., 2022;Yilmaz & Yilmaz, 2023), and affectivemotivational dimensions such as interest and engagement (Du & Daniel, 2024;Liang & Hwang, 2023). \n\nHowever, much of this work focuses on the functional roles of a single generative AI agent in assisting learners, often in task-specific or short-term settings. Fewer studies delve into the interactional nuances-how students engage with AI over time, how they shift roles in dialogic processes, and how they coordinate meaning-making across different learning contexts. Urban et al. (2024), for instance, found that students perceive ChatGPT interactions as natural and low-pressure, resembling communication with a human teacher but without the associated social risks.",
