        "text": "ChatGPT is part of a family of Open AI (Artificial Intelligence) technologies known as Large Language Models (LLMs) . 1 The recent popularity of LLMs is based on their ability to provide information to users based on large knowledge bases of facts, and applications of both supervised and reinforcement machine learning techniques, in order to make inferences and combine pieces of related information. Since the release of ChatGPT, there has been much speculation and experimentation regarding its potential to make a profound impact on business, education, and society [ 1 ]. \n\nGartner estimates that Generative AI, based on LLMs, will lead to a productivity revolution in the next decade [ 2 ]. Dwivedi et al. [ 3 ] note that LLMs will be disruptive in all domains because of their potential to automate mundane and repetitive tasks. Many early adopters are using Chat-GPT in education. Initial studies reveal that public discourse in social media is generally positive, with enthusiasm regarding its use in educational settings [ 4 ]. LLMs are observed to serve as a personalized support assistant with real-time feedback for students of all ages and backgrounds [ 5 -8 ]. However, researchers caution that ChatGPT's use in education could also aggravate various challenges, including work avoidance, cheating, manipulation, dishonesty, and a lack of truthfulness about ChatGPT use. It could also affect privacy and produce misleading results. Moreover, the usefulness of these technologies can be impacted by technological anchors (computer selfefficacy, computer anxiety, computer playfulness) that are associated with any new technology, thereby impacting adoption and use [ 9 ]. \n\nAlthough early studies on the use of ChatGPT examine its benefits for teaching and learning, as well as its responsible implementation [ 4 ], the impact of providing LLMs as a form of technology support for student learning outcomes and its effect on student goals and well-being has received little attention. Initial research confirms that LLMs provide students with social support, which has been provided by peers or instructors in a traditional education setting. For example, Margono et al. [ 5 ] note that LLMs can replace peers and instructors, who previously assisted students with any academic work.",
