      "snippet": {
        "text": "Large language models (LLMs) provide new opportunities for instructor-facing AI tools, including automating instructional design, evaluating student work, and generating adaptive teaching recommendations [7]. However, research has primarily focused on student-facing applications, with limited exploration of LLMs as pedagogical support for instructors, particularly those with low AI literacy [9]. LLMs have been used to evaluate student-generated content [13], simulate student responses to aid question evaluation [1], and fine-tune chatbots for early math education [14]. These studies reveal LLM's ability to support content development, but challenges remain in contextual alignment with instructor needs [15] [7]. Other studies user LLM as backbone for chat-based and quiz-based AI tutoring [12], \n\nTo support instructional decision-making, LLM-driven learning analytics dashboards provide insights into student-AI interactions, detect misuse, and assist in assessment refinement [16] [2]. However, these systems often assume AI literacy and barely mention AI attitude, limiting their usefulness for a broad spectrum of instructors. Research on AI prompt engineering tools such as ChainForge [17] and EvalLM [10] imply the importance of iterative refinement to enhance AIgenerated outputs. Similarly, studies on intelligent tutoring systems emphasize the role of instructors in co-designing AI feedback mechanisms, ensuring alignment with pedagogical goals [11]. Other research on AI in assessment underscores the need for transparent, trust-building mechanisms [9]. \n\nThis study advances prior research by designing an LLM-powered pedagogy agent in close collaboration with pedagogy experts-a domain expertise underexplored in LLM research. Often based in U.S. teaching centers, these experts drive instructional support, curriculum reform, and pedagogical innovation [20], making them key partners in shaping AI-based teaching tools.",
