      "snippet": {
        "text": "Artificial intelligence (AI) is the simulation of human intelligence processes by machines or programs to solve problems ranging from medical diagnoses to loan application processing. \n\nMore recently, chatbot technology has emerged in a sophisticated enough manner to facilitate humanlike conversations-a subfield known as generative AI (GenAI). OpenAI's ChatGPT (generative pretrained transformer) is among the most prominent of these chatbots (Zhu et al., 2023). With its explosion in userbase over the last couple of years, millions of people around the world turn to chatbots such as ChatGPT to answer questions and find information (Halaweh, 2023). Although AI tools have been emerging in the last two decades, some researchers state that recently released GenAI technologies may change the landscape of education and have the potential to revolutionize how we teach and learn (Dawson, 2023;Volante et al., 2023b). \n\nHowever, despite its affordances, concerns around ChatGPT and other GenAI tools have emerged, including academic integrity, accuracy, and bias of information (Kumar, 2023;Trust et al., 2023). Accordingly, Kumar et al. (2023) and Zawacki-Richter et al. (2019) emphasize the need for critical reflections and discussions on the use of AI in education (AIEd). Given these research highlights and acknowledging educators' varied levels of awareness and conflicting views toward AIEd, two teacher educators (the authors of this paper) in the Faculty of Education at Brock University, Canada, facilitated workshops among different groups of faculty instructors. The workshops focused on the emerging nature of GenAI tools, their affordances, and their implications for educators' practices. In this paper, the authors describe the details of these workshops, present their reflections, and discuss future implications.",
