      "snippet": {
        "text": "Generating faulty math problems is not only inherently a creative process, but it is also unconventional, as it is not a typical human task. Our initial approach involved enlisting American high school math competition (AMC) participants to create faulty math problems from scratch. However, this effort resulted in a problem set that is biased toward particular mathematical categories and specific types of falsehoods. We then explored data augmentation via LLMs, using this seed dataset created by high-school students, but this approach also failed. Inspired by recent studies on generating synthetic data using LLMs (Lou et al., 2024;Yuan et al., 2023), we extensively experimented with modifying valid problems from the MATH dataset using GPT-4 by giving it well-crafted prompts. This approach significantly improved the likelihood of generating genuinely false math problems. \n\nWe began by generating a pool of raw false math problems using GPT-4. After a carefully designed filtering process conducted by GPT-4, human evaluators assessed the falsehood of these problems. \n\nThe entire process is summarized in Figure 2, with details provided in the following section.",
