        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "In terms of the \"mathematical capabilities\" of ChatGPT, Frieder et al. (2023) investigated the use of two iterations of ChatGPT (released January 9, 2023 and January 30, 2023) and of GPT-4 by testing them on publicly available datasets, as well as hand-crafted ones, at undergraduate and graduate level mathematics. They claimed that \"ChatGPT can be used most successfully as a mathematical assistant for querying facts, acting as a mathematical search engine and knowledge base interface\" (p. 1). Further, GPT-4 seemed to be useful for undergraduate-level mathematics but failed on graduate-level difficulty, and in terms of ChatGPT's examination-solving abilities, the overall mathematical performance did not reach the level of a graduate student. Other studies also explored the capabilities of generative AI technologies in general in producing accurate mathematical solutions (Buchholtz et al., 2024;Hendrycks et al., 2021;Lewkowycz et al., 2022). Despite advancements in quality, issues with LLMs' hallucinations persist, posing ongoing challenges (Plevris et al., 2023;Rawte et al., 2023;Schorcht et al., 2023). \n\nIn a study before the availability of ChatGPT, Gadanidis (2017) investigated the intersection of AI, computational thinking (CT), and mathematics education for young students (K-8). In particular, he focused on three key elements that are common to AI, computational thinking, and mathematics education: agency, modeling of phenomena (see also Molina-Toro et al., 2019, for digital technology and modeling), and abstract concepts beyond specific instances. He claimed that \"seeing mathematics education through the lenses of other disciplines and recognizing that there is a significant overlap of key elements reinforces the importance of agency, modeling, and abstraction in mathematics education and provides new contexts and tools for incorporating them in classroom practice\" (p. 133).",
