      "snippet": {
        "text": "The use of educational technologies has been claimed to transform teacher and student learning environments in mathematics education (e.g., Pepin et al., 2024a). Those technologies that are based on artificial intelligence (AI) and that incorporate elements of machine learning are becoming common across the educational terrain (Celik et al., 2022;Crompton et al., 2024;Kasneci et al., 2023;Miao & Holmes, 2023;Zhang & Tur, 2024), including mathematics education. For example, various AI technologies are used in intelligent tutoring systems (ITS), automated assessment, and adaptive learning technologies (Almarashdi et al., 2024;Engelbrecht & Borba, 2024;Hershkovitz et al., 2024;Hwang & Tu, 2021;Lee & Yeo, 2022;Luzano, 2024;Melchor et al., 2023;Yeo, et al., 2024). \n\nDeep neural networks are a specialized subset of machine learning. These multilayered networks of nodes (called neurons in reference to the human brain) are capable of recognizing complex patterns in large datasets. Deep neural networks form the basis for many modern AI applications, including speech and image recognition systems. One specialty is language models developed specifically for processing and generating human speech (Floridi & Chiriatti, 2020). They can (to a certain extent) understand, generate, and translate text. Particularly noteworthy here are the Transformer models (Devlin et al., 2018), which are based on an architecture that allows processing long dependencies in texts and generating contextually relevant responses (Generative Pretrained Transformer, GPT). The most advanced language models currently include large language models (LLMs), such as GPT-4 or GPT-4o, Gemini 1.5, or Claude 3, on which the conversational agent ChatGPT is also based. These models are trained on large datasets to generate human-like text and perform a variety of tasks, such as text summarization, translation, and dialog management (Floridi & Chiriatti, 2020).",
