      "snippet": {
        "text": "Chatbots1 are now more present than they have ever been. Although first versions were developed many decades ago (Bii et al., 2018), a recent literature review demonstrates that very little research has been done so far regarding chatbots in mathematics education (Kuhail et al., 2023). This is likely to change as bots like ChatGPT are now widely accessible and can be used to communicate about almost anything including mathematical topics. Thus, integrating chatbots in mathematics education seems to be an obvious next step and one that is also being discussed by the Standing Conference of the Ministers of Education and Cultural Affairs of the Länder in the Federal Republic of Germany (SWK, 2024), which states that large language models should also be part of secondary schools. This requires the necessary competence to be developed for integrating large language models like ChatGPT in instruction (Ausat et al., 2023). Therefore, we consider it as one of our tasks as teacher educators to prepare future teachers for this task. \n\nSince there is evidence that teaching (with) technology (and large language models like ChatGPT as a specific technology) requires domain-specific professional competence (Gerber et al., 2023;Siller et al., 2023), we approach this study with a focus on mathematics education. For this matter, we firstly discuss potentials and challenges of large language models in education, before we go into providing a theoretical framework. This includes a discussion of the most influential models for teacher knowledge as part of professional teacher competence. Based on this, we conducted a study to analyze teacher knowledge regarding relevant aspects of teaching with large language models. This is done to be able to provide teacher training that is tailored to the needs and capabilities of our pre-service teachers.",
