      "snippet": {
        "text": "LLMs have shown promise in the generation of diverse educational materials [21], including learning objectives, worked examples [22,23], teaching questions, and complete course content [24][25][26][27][28][29]. A growing body of research demonstrates the potential for LLMs to improve learning outcomes in areas such as teacher coaching [30][31][32], student support [33,34], and content development [35,36]. In math education, LLMs have generated and adapted math problems at various difficulty levels with the goal of enhancing student understanding [37,38]. \n\nLarge math datasets with student interactions exist, such as CIMA from [39] or Math-Dial from [40], but they are created from synthetic sources. Lower pedagogical quality responses have resulted from the use of such datasets [41,42]. Our work uses real interactions between math learning researchers and real students. Additionally, in this study, we have included the feedback from math teachers on our dataset for future improvement. \n\nOur aim is to help algebra educators better identify misconceptions and errors (MaEs) in the classroom. This focus is particularly important given that passing the 6th grade math is a strong predictor of high school graduation [43]. The primary research question addressed in this study is the following: \n\nHow can AI solutions help educators identify students' misconceptions? \n\nThe complementary questions to be answered with this research are: \n\n• Which specific misconceptions in algebra are most frequently observed among students, and how can AI be trained to detect them reliably? • How does the inclusion of these identified misconceptions in AI-driven models enhance the detection and early intervention process? • What types of actionable feedback can AI provide to help educators address and correct these misconceptions effectively? \n\nWe propose a three-part solution: \n\n1. The creation of a comprehensive algebra misconception benchmark for 4th-8th grade 