      "snippet": {
        "text": "One major limitation is the small sample size, which may have affected the statistical power of the results, requiring caution in generalizing the findings. In addition, the study assessed only immediate post-intervention effects, without follow-up assessments to measure long-term retention or the practical application of learned skills. This constraint, shaped by the semester-long course structure, restricted the ability to evaluate whether students continued to use LLMs and CAS effectively beyond the intervention. Furthermore, the study utilized general-purpose LLMs, which were not specifically tailored for mathematical education, potentially limiting their effectiveness. \n\nFuture research should address these limitations by incorporating longitudinal evaluations and expanding sample diversity across different institutional contexts. Extending data collection to include long-term assessments would provide deeper insights into the sustained impact of LLMs and CAS in mathematics education. In addition, further studies could explore the effects of varying the duration and intensity of interventions to assess whether extended exposure and comprehensive training enhance students' familiarity, confidence, and effective use of LLMs. Examining the impact of advanced prompt-engineering strategies may offer insights into how training in precisiondriven AI interaction improves students' problem-solving abilities. Furthermore, exploring the potential of customized LLMs tailored for mathematical education could provide targeted support for students with diverse learning needs. \n\nWhile the integration of LLMs presents substantial educational opportunities, their responsible and ethical use remains essential. \n\nStudents must approach LLM-generated outputs with caution, verifying results to ensure alignment with intended learning objectives. Educators play a crucial role in guiding the appropriate use of these tools, fostering critical engagement rather than passive reliance. GenAI should be viewed as a supplementary resource, with its outputs treated as initial insights subject to careful review and refinement. Adhering to guidelines and frameworks such as UNESCO's \"Guidance for Generative AI in Education and Research\" [50] can support the ethical and effective integration of these technologies, preserving educational integrity while unlocking their full potential. \n\nQ2. Performing arithmetic operations with an LLM -Provide various arithmetic operations to an LLM. \n\nThen, copy and paste the question and the response into the space below.",
