      "snippet": {
        "text": "AI is the simulation of human integration processes through computer systems (Zhu et al., 2023) and its use is increasingly frequent. The use of Chatbots in education has become a popular tool in university education (Pérez & Robador Papich, 2023). Chatbots or virtual assistants can carry out human-like conversations and generating different results such as ideas and reports, among others (Zhu et al., 2023). \n\nChatGPT is a generative artificial intelligence (AI) chatbot, launched in 30 November 2022 by the research organization Open AI. The latest version is ChatGPT-4, released on 4 March 2023, only for ChatGPT Plus subscribers, which has more than 1 billion parameters, which increases the possibility of providing accurate answers to questions posed by users. \n\nThe use of ChatGPT in the educational sector has generated controversy, since on one hand there are those who assume that it is an opportunity for learning, and on the other hand, those who see it as a threat (Grassini, 2023). \n\nVarious universities have used ChatGPT in their educational programs, such as Cornell University, Stanford University, Universidad Nacional del Sur in Argentina and Universidad Nacional de Córdoba in Argentina (Pérez & Robador Papich, 2023), in all of them, Chatbots have improved academic performance, such as improving writing skills, defining concepts and definitions. Although on the other hand, there are educational institutions that have prohibited its use. \n\nChatGPT has demonstrated the ability to maintain conversations in a colloquial manner, which makes it very attractive and with great potential (Pérez & Robador Papich, 2023), in addition to demonstrating superior performance in understanding complex questions and producing plausible answers (Zhu et al., 2023) which allows education to be personalized, adaptive (Davis & Roblyer, 2021), accessible and flexible, encouraging the student's autonomous work (Pérez & Robador Papich, 2023).",
