      "snippet": {
        "text": "They analyzed 36 studies, conducting three experiments to detect countries, methods, and participants, with 90%, 80%, and 68.75% accuracy. The results of this study show that LLMs can perform scientific content analysis tasks, suggesting their usefulness in research that tolerates certain inaccuracies. The study provides empirical evidence on the applicability of LLMs in academic content analysis. \n\nIn a study conducted in Sri Lanka, Generative AI has demonstrated improved coding proficiency among university students, showing an average learning gain (N-Gain = 0.33) [28]. It is important to note that personalized interventions based on demographic variables may foster inclusive and effective programming learning environments. However, this study presents limitations, such as not disclosing the sample size or not employing a control group that would allow for a comparison of the intervention's effects. \n\nIn the study by [29], it is determined that generative AI can significantly enhance several aspects of programming education by assisting instructors in preparing materials, providing personalized content and feedback to students, and supporting course evaluation with generated questions. This was validated through a specific use case in a Java programming course, where the aim was to identify competencies in each problem set to clarify learning objectives, develop a chatbot aligned with course materials to serve as a tutor for students, and use a GenAI tool to generate questions. Despite these advantages, the study takes an exploratory approach rather than a controlled experiment, focusing on practical applications and initial implementations of GenAI in an educational environment, and needs to be more conclusive. \n\nThe research by [5] explores how students in Ireland use generative AI tools like ChatGPT while learning to program in Python, to improve pedagogical strategies to enhance learning experiences. This study seeks to fill the research gap and will analyze how a purposive sample of postgraduate computer science students interacts with these artificial intelligence tools. Given its qualitative nature, the study focuses on student experiences rather than on effective learning, emphasizing the exploration of student engagement with AI tools in an educational context. \n\nDespite the potential benefits of generative artificial intelligence in programming learning, some studies have identified challenges. For instance, [3] studied the impact of GenAI on active learning, focusing on large language models (LLMs) in education.",
