      "snippet": {
        "text": "However, some theory and mounting evidence support a \"fade-out\" model, where individual differences in EF skills become less relevant in upper elementary school, compared to earlier grades, as differences in domain knowledge play a much stronger role in supporting learning (Stipek & Valentino, 2015;Zhang et al., 2023). EF skills at age 5 predicted students' math achievement at age 5 and growth in math achievement from age 5 to 14, but the influence of EF skills was much stronger in early elementary grades than later grades (Stipek & Valentino, 2015). Individual differences in EF skills were often not predictive of math achievement in late elementary or secondary school after controlling for prior math achievement (Ahmed et al., 2019;Geary et al., 2017;Watts et al., 2015). We hypothesized that these findings with predominantly White, middle-class students would generalize to our sample, such that students' EF skills in middle school would not predict math achievement in high school after controlling for prior math achievement. \n\nPrior math achievement. Math achievement captures individual differences in math knowledge, past success learning from the informal and formal math experiences, and ability to demonstrate this knowledge in a standardized testing environment. In part, relations between prior and future math achievement may reflect how new math concepts and procedures build on past concepts and procedures, so prior knowledge is needed to successfully learn new content. In part, it also may reflect other stable individual (e.g., personality and cognitive skills) and environmental (e.g., poverty and discrimination) characteristics that influence mathematics achievement (Bailey et al., 2014;Watts et al., 2017). Math achievement around school entry and in the middle grades are strong predictors of later math achievement, and math knowledge in fifth grade helped mediate the relation between first-grade and age 15 math achievement (Bailey et al., 2014;Siegler et al., 2012;Watts et al., 2015). \n\nIn the current study, we considered three components of math achievement in middle school that are emphasized in state math standards (e.g., National Governors Assocation Center for Best Practices, 2010) and on college-readiness assessments (ACT, 2022).",
